export const DATA_35: ReadingTest[] = [
  { id: "rd-042", num: 42, title: "Đề 042", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "Songs from Truong Sa", paragraphs: ["For many Vietnamese people, the name Truong Sa — the Spratly Islands in the South China Sea — carries a weight that goes far beyond geography. It is a place associated with duty, sacrifice, and a deep sense of national belonging. Over the past four decades, Vietnamese musicians and poets have responded to this emotional significance by creating a body of songs dedicated to the soldiers and sailors stationed on the islands. These songs, collectively known as \"nhac Truong Sa\" or \"Truong Sa music\", have become one of the most beloved and enduring traditions in Vietnamese popular culture.", "The earliest songs in this tradition appeared in the 1970s and 1980s, when Vietnam was still recovering from the effects of war and the nation's maritime borders were a source of constant concern. Composers who visited the islands or worked closely with military personnel began writing pieces that captured the loneliness, courage, and pride of life on a remote outpost surrounded by open sea. Many of these early songs relied on simple, lyrical melodies that were easy for soldiers to sing together — a quality that helped them spread quickly through military units and, eventually, into the broader public.", "One of the most celebrated composers in this tradition is Thanh Tung, whose song \"Bien Van\" (\"The Sea Still Calling\") has remained a favourite for over three decades. Thanh Tung has spoken in interviews about the experience of visiting Truong Sa and being deeply moved by the sight of young soldiers standing watch over the vast, open water, far from their families. His music tries to convey both the hardship of that isolation and the quiet dignity with which the soldiers bear it. \"I did not want to write heroic anthems,\" he has said, \"but something that felt true to the human experience of being there.\"", "The themes of Truong Sa music are remarkably consistent across different composers and different decades. Love for the homeland, longing for family, the beauty and severity of the sea, and the bond between soldiers are the subjects that appear again and again. Many songs are written from the perspective of a soldier addressing a loved one back home, or from the perspective of a person on shore imagining their distant partner keeping watch over the islands. This dual perspective — land and sea, waiting and guarding — gives the tradition much of its emotional power.", "In recent years, Truong Sa music has attracted a new generation of listeners who discover the songs through online video platforms and social media. Young Vietnamese musicians have begun recording fresh versions of classic Truong Sa songs, sometimes blending traditional melodies with contemporary arrangements. These updated recordings have introduced the tradition to listeners who might otherwise have had no connection to it, while also sparking debate among older fans about whether such adaptations honour or dilute the original spirit of the music.", "Concerts dedicated to Truong Sa music are held regularly in major Vietnamese cities, often organised around national anniversaries or days of significance for the armed forces. These events frequently feature not only professional performers but also veterans and serving military personnel who participate as singers or audience members. The atmosphere at such concerts is described by attendees as deeply moving — a rare public space in which personal emotion, collective memory, and national pride are openly and unselfconsciously expressed.", "Beyond entertainment, Truong Sa music serves a function that is difficult to quantify . It keeps alive an awareness of the islands and the people who defend them among a civilian population that might otherwise remain distant from the realities of maritime service. It reminds listeners that behind the political and territorial disputes that periodically appear in news headlines, there are real individuals living and working in extraordinary conditions. In this sense, the songs are not just music — they are a form of cultural memory, stitching the islands into the emotional fabric of the nation."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Truong Sa music was created primarily to support military recruitment efforts in Vietnam.", "The popularity of Truong Sa music has declined since the introduction of online music platforms.", "Truong Sa music is a culturally significant tradition that connects Vietnamese people to the islands and those who defend them.", "Composer Thanh Tung is the most important figure in the development of Vietnamese patriotic music."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what helped the earliest Truong Sa songs spread widely among the public?", options: ["Government authorities distributed printed songbooks to military units.", "They were broadcast regularly on national television and radio programmes.", "Their simple and expressive melodies made them easy for soldiers to sing together.", "They were composed by musicians who had personally served in the military."], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"lyrical\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["complex and technically demanding, requiring trained musicians", "repetitive and rhythmic, suitable for marching in formation", "expressive and melodious, conveying deep personal feeling", "loud and forceful, designed to inspire courage in battle"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did composer Thanh Tung say about his intentions when writing Truong Sa music?", options: ["He aimed to write dramatic anthems that would celebrate the bravery of Vietnamese soldiers.", "He wanted to create music that would be performed at major national ceremonies.", "He hoped his music would encourage young Vietnamese people to join the armed forces.", "He sought to capture the genuine human experience of life on the islands rather than produce heroic songs."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what gives Truong Sa music much of its emotional power?", options: ["The fact that the songs are written by composers who have personally experienced life at sea", "The use of traditional Vietnamese instruments that evoke a strong sense of history", "The inclusion of real events from the lives of specific soldiers stationed on the islands", "The way songs present both the perspective of those keeping watch and those waiting on shore"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how has Truong Sa music reached a new generation of listeners in recent years?", options: ["Through school music programmes that include Truong Sa songs in the national curriculum", "Through concerts held at international venues to promote Vietnamese culture abroad", "Through online video platforms and social media where fresh recordings are shared", "Through government-sponsored radio broadcasts targeting listeners under the age of thirty"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["a person on shore imagining a distant loved one on the islands", "the Vietnamese musicians who record Truong Sa songs", "the soldiers stationed on the Truong Sa islands", "the composers who write songs about maritime life"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"quantify\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to measure or express in specific numerical terms", "to compare with examples from other cultures", "to justify through historical evidence", "to describe in vivid and emotional terms"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a feature of Truong Sa music concerts?", options: ["They often take place on dates connected to national or military significance.", "Veterans and serving military personnel sometimes participate as performers or audience members.", "They are held regularly in major Vietnamese cities.", "Tickets for these concerts are provided free of charge to members of the armed forces."], answer: 3 }, { q: "What is the author's overall attitude toward the tradition of Truong Sa music?", options: ["Critical — the author questions whether the music accurately represents life on the islands.", "Neutral — the author presents information without expressing any personal evaluation.", "Uncertain — the author is unsure whether the tradition will survive into the future.", "Admiring — the author treats Truong Sa music as a meaningful and valuable cultural tradition."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "A Shoe Company with a Social Mission", paragraphs: ["In 2006, a young American entrepreneur named Blake Mycoskie was travelling in Argentina when he noticed a striking problem. Many children in rural villages had no shoes, leaving their feet exposed to injury, soil-transmitted parasites, and the sharp terrain of their environment. Local health workers explained to him that this was not simply a matter of poverty — it was also a barrier to schooling, since many schools required children to wear footwear before they could attend. Mycoskie returned home with a clear intention: to build a business that would provide shoes to those who needed them, not as a charity, but through a sustainable commercial model.", "The company he founded, TOMS Shoes, was built around what he called the \"One for One\" model. For every pair of shoes sold to a consumer, TOMS would donate a pair to a child in need. It was an elegantly simple idea, and it proved to have extraordinary commercial appeal. By linking the purchase of a product to a direct act of giving, Mycoskie tapped into a growing desire among consumers — particularly younger, more socially conscious buyers — to make their spending choices reflect their values. Within the first year, TOMS had donated over ten thousand pairs of shoes; within a decade, that number had surpassed seventy million.", "The model TOMS pioneered is now commonly known as \"buy-one-give-one\" or B1G1, and it has been adopted by a wide range of companies across different industries. Eyewear brands have used it to fund cataract surgeries in developing countries. Coffee companies have linked each bag sold to the planting of trees. Bag manufacturers have donated school supplies for every backpack purchased. The underlying principle — that profit and social good need not be in conflict — has become one of the defining ideas of what commentators often call \"conscious capitalism\" or the social enterprise movement.", "However, the TOMS model has also attracted substantial criticism. Development economists and aid specialists have argued that donating goods produced in wealthier countries can undermine local industries in the communities it is intended to help. When free shoes flood a market, local cobblers and shoe manufacturers lose customers, potentially destroying livelihoods in the very communities the donations are meant to benefit. This argument is part of a broader debate in international development about the unintended consequences of in-kind donations versus cash transfers, which give recipients the freedom to purchase what they actually need from local suppliers.", "TOMS responded to these criticisms over time by modifying its approach. The company began sourcing a portion of its donated shoes from manufacturers within the recipient countries, thereby directing economic activity toward local producers. It also expanded its giving model beyond footwear: by 2014, it had introduced programmes providing safe drinking water, maternal health services, and eye care. This evolution reflected a growing recognition within the company that the complexity of poverty required responses more sophisticated than a simple product donation.", "The story of TOMS raises broader questions about the relationship between business and social responsibility. Traditional models of corporate philanthropy involved companies donating a portion of their profits to charitable causes as a separate activity from their core commercial operations. The TOMS model, by contrast, embedded social impact directly into the commercial transaction itself. Critics of this approach argue that it allows consumers to feel good about their purchases without demanding genuine systemic change, and that it can serve primarily as a marketing tool that benefits the company's brand rather than addressing root causes of poverty.", "Supporters counter that even imperfect models of giving are preferable to none, and that companies like TOMS have succeeded in directing hundreds of millions of dollars in resources toward communities in need while simultaneously building commercially successful enterprises. The debate remains unresolved. What is clear is that TOMS fundamentally changed expectations about what a consumer goods company could and should do — introducing a generation of entrepreneurs and shoppers alike to the idea that buying a pair of shoes could mean something beyond the transaction itself."], questions: [{ q: "What is the main idea of the passage?", options: ["Donating goods to developing countries is always more effective than transferring cash to local communities.", "TOMS Shoes introduced an influential model linking commerce and social impact, which has generated both admiration and debate.", "Blake Mycoskie is the most successful social entrepreneur of the twenty-first century.", "The buy-one-give-one model is the most effective way for companies to address global poverty."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, why did the \"One for One\" model prove so commercially successful?", options: ["It connected purchasing decisions to a direct act of giving, appealing to socially conscious consumers.", "It offered products at lower prices than competing shoe brands in the same market.", "It was promoted by celebrity endorsements that attracted widespread media attention.", "It was supported by large government grants that allowed TOMS to reduce production costs."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"reflect\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to produce an exact copy of an original object", "to reconsider a past decision carefully before acting", "to delay a response in order to gather more information", "to express or demonstrate something outwardly"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what criticism did development economists raise about the TOMS model?", options: ["TOMS failed to consult local communities before deciding which goods to donate.", "The cost of transporting shoes to remote areas made the model financially unsustainable.", "The quality of donated shoes was too poor to provide meaningful protection for children's feet.", "Donating imported goods could damage local businesses and destroy jobs in recipient communities."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did TOMS change its approach in response to criticism?", options: ["It reduced the price of its consumer products to allow more people to participate in the giving model.", "It stopped donating shoes entirely and redirected all resources toward cash transfer programmes.", "It partnered with international aid organisations to manage all future donations on its behalf.", "It began sourcing donated shoes locally and broadened its giving to include health and water programmes."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"undermine\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to weaken or damage gradually", "to replace entirely with a more effective alternative", "to investigate and expose hidden faults", "to strengthen and expand through steady investment"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, how does the TOMS model differ from traditional corporate philanthropy?", options: ["Traditional philanthropy focuses on environmental causes, while TOMS focuses on individual welfare.", "Traditional philanthropy requires consumers to opt in to giving, while TOMS donates automatically.", "Traditional philanthropy donates only to registered charities, while TOMS works with governments.", "Traditional philanthropy separates giving from commerce, while TOMS embeds social impact within the sale itself."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"adopted\" in paragraph 3 refers to what has been adopted by other companies?", options: ["The specific shoe designs that TOMS used in its original Argentine donation programme", "The partnership agreements that TOMS established with governments in developing countries", "The buy-one-give-one model that TOMS pioneered", "The marketing strategy that TOMS used to appeal to younger consumers"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a change TOMS made to its model after facing criticism?", options: ["It introduced a cash transfer scheme giving recipients money to buy from local markets.", "It began sourcing a portion of donated shoes from manufacturers in recipient countries.", "It introduced a programme providing safe drinking water.", "It launched maternal health and eye care programmes."], answer: 0 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 6 and 7, what can be inferred about the author's overall view of the TOMS model?", options: ["The author believes the model is fundamentally flawed and should be abandoned by the business community.", "The author thinks the model is perfect and that critics have failed to understand its true impact.", "The author is convinced that the model primarily benefits the company's marketing rather than communities in need.", "The author views the model as genuinely influential and net-positive, while acknowledging it is not without valid criticism."], answer: 3 }] },
    { title: "Investing in Climate Protection and Disaster Prevention", paragraphs: ["In recent decades, the frequency and severity of climate-related disasters — from catastrophic floods and prolonged droughts to destructive wildfires and powerful tropical storms — have increased at a rate that has alarmed scientists, policymakers, and communities around the world. The human and economic costs of these events have been staggering. According to estimates from leading international bodies, natural disasters linked to climate change cause losses of hundreds of billions of dollars every year, a figure that is projected to rise sharply as global temperatures continue to climb. Against this backdrop, the question of how to invest in climate protection and disaster prevention has moved from the margins of policy debate to its very centre.", "For much of the twentieth century, governments approached disaster management primarily through a reactive lens, channelling resources into emergency response and post-disaster reconstruction rather than prevention. This model, while understandable given the uncertainties of early climate science, has proven costly in the long run. A widely cited study by the United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction found that every dollar invested in disaster risk reduction saves approximately six dollars in future losses. The case for shifting resources upstream — from response to prevention — is therefore not merely ethical but overwhelmingly economic.", "Climate adaptation investments take many forms. At the infrastructure level, they include the construction of sea walls, flood barriers, and elevated road networks in vulnerable coastal areas, as well as the retrofitting of existing buildings to withstand extreme heat or seismic activity. At the ecosystem level, investments in the restoration of mangrove forests, wetlands, and urban green spaces can provide natural buffers against storm surges, reduce surface runoff, and moderate the urban heat island effect. In the agricultural sector, funding directed at drought-resistant crop varieties, improved irrigation systems, and early warning technologies helps communities maintain food security under increasingly unpredictable climatic conditions.", "Among the countries that have most effectively integrated disaster prevention into national development planning is Bangladesh. Widely regarded as one of the nations most exposed to climate risk, Bangladesh has invested heavily in a network of coastal embankments, cyclone shelters, and community-based early warning systems over the past three decades. The results have been remarkable: despite facing cyclones of comparable or greater intensity than those which caused tens of thousands of deaths in the 1970s, Bangladesh now regularly reports death tolls in the hundreds rather than the thousands. This dramatic reduction in mortality has been attributed not to any decrease in storm intensity but to systematic investment in preparedness infrastructure and community resilience.", "Despite the clear evidence in favour of prevention, significant barriers to adequate investment remain. In many low- and middle-income countries, governments face competing fiscal pressures that make it difficult to allocate resources to long-term risk reduction when immediate developmental needs — healthcare, education, poverty alleviation — are urgent. The international climate finance system, which was designed in part to address this imbalance, has been criticised for directing too large a share of available funds toward mitigation — that is, reducing greenhouse gas emissions — and too small a share toward adaptation and disaster resilience. Developing countries, which bear a disproportionate share of climate risk despite contributing least to global emissions, have argued consistently and forcefully that this allocation must be rebalanced.", "The private sector, too, has a critical role to play. Insurance companies, pension funds, and development banks are increasingly recognising that unpriced climate risk represents a material threat to their portfolios and are beginning to factor disaster exposure into investment decisions. Green bonds, catastrophe bonds, and parametric insurance instruments are among the financial innovations that have emerged to channel private capital toward climate resilience. While these mechanisms remain nascent in many markets, their growth signals a broader recognition that the costs of inaction far exceed the costs of preventive investment.", "What is becoming increasingly clear is that climate protection and disaster prevention are not optional expenditures but foundational investments in the stability and productivity of economies and societies. [1] Regions that fail to build resilience today are likely to face not only escalating disaster losses but also declining investor confidence, population displacement, and long-term erosion of productive capacity. [2] Conversely, the countries and cities that invest strategically in climate-proof infrastructure, early warning systems, and ecosystem restoration are positioning themselves for sustainable growth in an era of deepening environmental uncertainty. [3] The transition from a reactive to a proactive approach to disaster risk is, in this sense, among the most consequential economic choices that governments and institutions face in the twenty-first century. [4]"], questions: [{ q: "What is the main argument of the passage?", options: ["International climate finance has successfully redirected sufficient resources toward adaptation in the most vulnerable nations.", "Investing proactively in climate protection and disaster prevention is both economically rational and essential for long-term societal stability.", "Private sector financial instruments are more effective than government policy in funding climate resilience.", "Developing countries should prioritise greenhouse gas mitigation over disaster preparedness to address the root causes of climate risk."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did a study by the United Nations Office for Disaster Risk Reduction find about investing in disaster risk reduction?", options: ["It reduces the frequency of natural disasters by approximately one third over a ten-year period.", "It requires a minimum of twenty years before measurable financial benefits are observed.", "It generates savings of around six dollars for every dollar spent on prevention.", "It is most effective when combined with post-disaster reconstruction funding."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a form of climate adaptation investment?", options: ["Developing satellite surveillance systems to monitor deforestation rates.", "Funding drought-resistant crop varieties and improved irrigation systems.", "Restoring mangrove forests and wetlands to buffer storm surges.", "Building sea walls and flood barriers in vulnerable coastal zones."], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["Climate adaptation investments at the infrastructure level.", "International bodies that fund disaster risk reduction programmes.", "The governments of vulnerable coastal nations.", "Local communities living in flood-prone areas."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what has been credited with dramatically reducing deaths from cyclones in Bangladesh?", options: ["International emergency aid delivered more rapidly following improved diplomatic ties.", "A measurable decrease in the intensity of tropical storms affecting the country.", "Relocation of coastal populations to inland urban centres over several decades.", "Systematic investment in preparedness infrastructure and community resilience."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, why do developing countries argue that the allocation of international climate finance must be rebalanced?", options: ["Because too much funding is currently directed at ecosystem restoration rather than infrastructure.", "Because they carry a disproportionate burden of climate risk while being among the smallest contributors to global emissions.", "Because the current system penalises countries that have already invested in disaster prevention infrastructure.", "Because mitigation funding is being used to subsidise fossil fuel industries in wealthy nations."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"costly\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Geographically limited in the areas where it can be applied.", "Technically complex and difficult to administer.", "Politically controversial among competing interest groups.", "Financially wasteful and economically damaging over time."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following financial instruments is mentioned as a way to direct private capital toward climate resilience?", options: ["Sovereign wealth funds managed by central banks in high-income countries.", "Microfinance loans distributed directly to farming communities in drought-prone regions.", "Government treasury bonds issued specifically for post-disaster reconstruction.", "Parametric insurance instruments designed to channel investment into climate preparedness."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Based on paragraphs 5 and 6 together, what can be inferred about how low-income countries are most likely to close the gap in climate resilience funding?", options: ["By focusing exclusively on mitigation measures that reduce emissions and thereby lower future disaster risk.", "Through a combination of reformed international climate finance that prioritises adaptation and growing private sector instruments such as green bonds and catastrophe bonds.", "By waiting for global temperatures to stabilise before committing to large-scale adaptation investments.", "By reducing spending on healthcare and education in order to redirect funds toward disaster prevention infrastructure."], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? \"The economic logic, then, is unambiguous: the cost of prevention is a fraction of the cost of recovery.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "Carbon Nanomaterials and the Future of Technology", paragraphs: ["The discovery of buckminsterfullerene in 1985 and the subsequent identification of carbon nanotubes in 1991 marked the beginning of a scientific revolution whose implications are still being worked out across dozens of fields. What these findings revealed was that carbon — an element long known for its versatile bonding chemistry — could be coaxed into structures of extraordinary geometrical precision at the nanoscale, and that these structures possessed properties so far removed from those of conventional carbon materials as to constitute, in effect, entirely new substances. [1] The decades that followed have seen the family of carbon nanomaterials expand to include graphene, carbon quantum dots, and a growing array of hybrid architectures, each offering a distinct combination of electrical, thermal, mechanical, and optical characteristics that conventional materials science struggled to anticipate.", "Graphene, a single-atom-thick sheet of carbon atoms arranged in a hexagonal lattice, is perhaps the most celebrated of these materials. First isolated in 2004 by Andre Geim and Konstantin Novoselov — work for which they received the Nobel Prize in Physics in 2010 — graphene exhibits a remarkable convergence of desirable properties: it is roughly two hundred times stronger than structural steel by weight, conducts electricity with lower resistivity than copper at room temperature, and transfers heat more efficiently than any other known material. These characteristics have prompted extensive research into its potential applications in next-generation electronics, flexible displays, energy storage devices, and high-performance filtration membranes.", "Carbon nanotubes, which can be conceptualised as graphene sheets rolled into seamless cylindrical forms, exhibit similarly exceptional properties, though the precise characteristics of a given nanotube depend critically on its diameter and the angle at which the graphene lattice is rolled — a parameter known as chirality. Depending on these structural variables, a carbon nanotube may behave as either a metallic conductor or a semiconductor, a distinction of considerable significance for transistor design. This tunability of electronic behaviour — achievable through controlled variation of physical geometry rather than chemical doping — represents a potential paradigm shift in the manufacture of nanoscale electronic components, though the challenge of producing chirality-specific nanotubes at industrial scale has so far constrained commercialisation.", "Beyond electronics, carbon nanomaterials have attracted intense interest in the biomedical field. Carbon quantum dots — nanoscale particles derived from carbon precursors and typically measuring fewer than ten nanometres in diameter — exhibit strong fluorescence across a tunable range of wavelengths, low cytotoxicity , and favourable surface chemistry for biological functionalisation. Their combination of optical brightness and biocompatibility has made them promising candidates for bioimaging applications, where they may eventually offer advantages over conventional heavy-metal-based quantum dots, whose toxicity poses significant barriers to clinical use. Functionalised carbon nanotubes, meanwhile, have been explored as vehicles for targeted drug delivery, with the capacity to penetrate cell membranes and release therapeutic payloads with a degree of spatial and temporal precision that conventional delivery mechanisms cannot readily achieve.", "The environmental and safety implications of carbon nanomaterials are, however, a subject of active and unresolved scientific debate. The very properties that make these materials so technologically attractive — their extreme surface-area-to-volume ratios, their chemical reactivity, and their capacity to interact with biological systems — arguably render them potentially hazardous if released into the environment or inhaled in occupational settings. Some studies have reported inflammatory responses in lung tissue exposed to carbon nanotubes of certain geometries, drawing comparisons — contested by other researchers — with the pathological mechanisms associated with asbestos fibres. Regulatory frameworks for nanomaterials remain underdeveloped in most jurisdictions, and the absence of standardised testing protocols has made it difficult to reach consensus on acceptable exposure thresholds.", "The commercialisation of carbon nanomaterials has proceeded unevenly across sectors. In energy storage, graphene-enhanced lithium-ion batteries and supercapacitors incorporating carbon nanotube electrodes have reached early commercial deployment, offering measurable improvements in charge density and cycle stability relative to conventional designs. In structural applications, carbon nanotube-reinforced composites are beginning to appear in aerospace and sporting goods, where the premium placed on strength-to-weight ratios justifies the currently elevated cost of nanomaterial incorporation. The electronics sector, by contrast, has found the path from laboratory demonstration to manufacturable product considerably more arduous, largely due to the unresolved challenges of precise nanoscale assembly and the integration of nanomaterials with established semiconductor fabrication processes.", "What the trajectory of carbon nanomaterials research suggests is that the transition from scientific curiosity to transformative technology is rarely linear and seldom rapid. [2] The properties that excited researchers in the 1990s remain genuinely extraordinary, and the breadth of potential applications — spanning energy, medicine, electronics, and structural engineering — is difficult to overstate. [3] Yet the distance between laboratory-scale demonstration and economically viable, safely deployable products has proven greater than early enthusiasm may have implied, and the field's most consequential contributions to everyday technology may still lie ahead. [4] In this respect, carbon nanomaterials exemplify a broader truth about transformative science: that the gap between discovery and deployment is itself a frontier — one that demands as much ingenuity, patience, and institutional commitment as the original act of discovery."], questions: [{ q: "Which of the following best captures the central argument of the passage?", options: ["Carbon nanomaterials have already transformed the electronics and biomedical industries and will soon replace all conventional materials.", "Graphene alone is sufficient to drive the next major revolution in materials science, making other carbon nanomaterials largely unnecessary.", "The primary obstacle to commercialising carbon nanomaterials is their toxicity, which has so far prevented regulatory approval in most countries.", "Carbon nanomaterials possess extraordinary properties with broad application potential, but the path from laboratory discovery to widespread deployment has proved slower and more complex than initially envisioned."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"celebrated\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["Commercially successful and broadly adopted across multiple industries.", "Controversial and subject to ongoing scientific dispute.", "Recently discovered and still under preliminary investigation.", "Widely admired and frequently highlighted for its remarkable qualities."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, what determines whether a carbon nanotube behaves as a metallic conductor or a semiconductor?", options: ["The chemical composition of the dopants introduced during the nanotube synthesis process.", "The temperature at which the nanotube was produced in the laboratory.", "The number of concentric walls present in the nanotube structure.", "The diameter and chirality — the angle at which the graphene lattice is rolled — of the nanotube."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"This\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["The use of chemical doping to alter the electrical properties of semiconductor materials.", "The ability to adjust the electronic behaviour of carbon nanotubes by varying their physical geometry.", "The challenge of producing chirality-specific nanotubes at industrial scale.", "The process of rolling graphene sheets into seamless cylindrical nanotube structures."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what makes carbon quantum dots potentially superior to conventional heavy-metal-based quantum dots for biomedical imaging?", options: ["Their strong fluorescence, low toxicity to cells, and biocompatible surface chemistry circumvent the clinical barriers posed by heavy-metal toxicity.", "They emit fluorescence exclusively in the visible light spectrum, making them easier to detect with standard clinical equipment.", "Carbon quantum dots are significantly cheaper to produce at industrial scale than heavy-metal alternatives.", "Carbon quantum dots can be injected directly into the bloodstream without requiring any prior chemical modification."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a sector where carbon nanomaterials have reached some level of commercial deployment?", options: ["Structural composites used in aerospace and sporting equipment.", "Energy storage, including graphene-enhanced lithium-ion batteries and nanotube supercapacitors.", "Early-stage electronics applications involving nanoscale assembly challenges.", "Water desalination membranes for large-scale municipal filtration systems."], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"them\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["The asbestos fibres with which certain carbon nanotube geometries have been compared.", "Carbon nanomaterials, whose very attractive properties may also carry potential environmental and health risks.", "The standardised testing protocols proposed by international health bodies.", "The regulatory frameworks governing nanomaterial safety testing."], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"cytotoxicity\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["The rate at which a nanomaterial dissolves when introduced into a biological fluid.", "The degree to which a substance emits light when stimulated by an external energy source.", "The level of harm or damage a substance causes to living cells.", "The capacity of a material to bond selectively to specific proteins on a cell surface."], answer: 2 }, { q: "Which of the following best describes the author's attitude toward the future impact of carbon nanomaterials, as expressed in paragraph 7?", options: ["Unequivocally optimistic, asserting that carbon nanomaterials will inevitably reshape every major industry within the next decade.", "Largely indifferent, implying that carbon nanomaterials are unlikely to offer advantages beyond those already achieved by conventional materials.", "Deeply pessimistic, suggesting that unresolved safety and manufacturing challenges will prevent these materials from ever reaching commercial viability.", "Cautiously measured, acknowledging genuine long-term potential while noting that the timeline to transformative impact has proven longer and more uncertain than initial enthusiasm suggested."], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? \"The history of materials science offers few examples of a substance moving seamlessly from discovery to widespread adoption without encountering unexpected obstacles along the way.\"", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 1 }] },
  ] },
  { id: "rd-078", num: 78, title: "Đề 078", timeMin: 60, parts: [
    { title: "A Visit to a Film Studio", paragraphs: ["Last summer, my family and I spent a day at Pinewood Studios, one of the most famous film production facilities in the United Kingdom. I had always been curious about how movies are made, so when my aunt — who works as a set designer — offered to arrange a behind-the-scenes tour, I was immediately excited. We arrived early in the morning, and even from the car park, the scale of the place was impressive. Enormous soundstages stretched across the site, and vehicles carrying equipment moved back and forth between buildings like a small, busy city.", "Our guide for the morning was a man named Colin, who had worked at the studio for over twenty years and clearly loved his job. He began by taking us into one of the soundstages, a huge indoor space where the ceiling was fitted with hundreds of lights pointing in every direction. Colin explained that these lights could be adjusted individually to create any atmosphere the director wanted — from bright sunshine to the soft glow of candlelight. In the center of the room, a partial set had been left in place from a recent production: a Victorian-era drawing room complete with period furniture, paintings on the walls, and props arranged to look as though someone had just stepped out for a moment.", "What surprised me most was how small the set actually was. From the outside of the soundstage, I had imagined vast, sweeping rooms; in reality, the drawing room was no bigger than my own living room at home. Colin laughed when I mentioned this and explained that cameras can make small spaces look enormous on screen, and that directors often choose to film in compact sets because they are easier to light and control. Every detail, however, was painstakingly accurate — the wallpaper pattern, the style of the clock on the mantelpiece, and even the crumbs on a plate had been chosen by the production designer to match the period precisely.", "After the soundstage, Colin led us to the props department, which was perhaps the most memorable part of the tour. The room was enormous — the size of an aircraft hangar — and filled from floor to ceiling with objects of every conceivable kind. Ancient Roman helmets sat beside modern office telephones; a full-sized wooden sailing ship occupied one corner, while rows of artificial plants lined another wall. Colin told us that many of the items had been used in multiple productions over the decades, and that the department employed a team of skilled craftspeople who could build, repair, or replicate almost any object on request within a matter of days.", "We also visited the costume department, where hundreds of outfits hung on long rails organized by period and style. A team of seamstresses was at work altering a military uniform for an upcoming production, and one of them — a cheerful woman named Diane — paused to show us the extraordinary level of detail that went into each garment. Even buttons were sourced to match historical records, and fabrics were sometimes artificially aged to look worn, faded, or weather-beaten before a single scene was filmed. Diane explained that the audience might never notice these details consciously, but that their absence would make costumes look unconvincing without anyone quite knowing why.", "By the time we said goodbye to Colin at the studio gates, I had completely changed my view of how films are made. I had expected technology and special effects to dominate everything, but the reality was something far more grounded: hundreds of skilled craftspeople working carefully and methodically to create the believable details that bring a story to life. My aunt smiled when I told her this and said it was exactly why she had loved her job for so many years. I left Pinewood with a deep new respect for the invisible work that makes movies feel real."], questions: [{ q: "What is the passage mainly about?", options: ["A comparison of different film studios in the United Kingdom and their production methods", "A guide to the different departments that work together during a major film production", "The history of Pinewood Studios and the famous films that have been produced there", "A personal account of a behind-the-scenes studio visit that changed the narrator's view of filmmaking"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how did the narrator's family get access to the studio tour?", options: ["The narrator's aunt, who works as a set designer, arranged the behind-the-scenes visit.", "They purchased tickets online through the studio's official visitor program.", "A friend who worked in the film industry recommended the studio and booked the tour.", "They entered a competition and won a guided tour as the prize."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what surprised the narrator most about the Victorian drawing room set?", options: ["It had been left in place from a very old production that had finished many years earlier.", "It was much smaller than expected, no bigger than the narrator's own living room.", "It had been built without any ceiling so that lighting equipment could be positioned above.", "The props inside had been made entirely from artificial materials to reduce production costs."], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the props department do when a production needs a specific object?", options: ["It borrows objects from museums and galleries under a special agreement with cultural institutions.", "It searches through auction houses and antique shops to find the original item.", "Its team of skilled craftspeople can build, repair, or reproduce almost any object within days.", "It sends a request to specialist suppliers in other countries and waits for delivery."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, why are fabrics in the costume department sometimes made to look old or worn before filming begins?", options: ["So that the costumes appear convincing and realistic on screen even if the audience does not consciously notice", "To save money by using cheaper materials that appear more authentic under studio lighting", "Because the director requests costumes that match the style of a particular historical era", "To ensure that the fabric does not reflect too much light when filmed under bright studio conditions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"they\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["compact sets, which are simpler to light and keep under control", "directors, who prefer working in confined spaces during filming", "cameras, which can make small spaces appear much larger on screen", "production designers, who choose every detail of the set carefully"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as an item found in the props department?", options: ["Rows of artificial plants", "A collection of vintage motor cars", "Ancient Roman helmets", "A full-sized wooden sailing ship"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"replicate\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["to create an exact copy of something", "to record the history and previous uses of a prop in detail", "to restore a damaged object to its original condition", "to display an item so that it looks more impressive on camera"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the seamstresses who alter and prepare the costumes for each production", "the garments hanging on rails in the costume department", "the fine details carefully incorporated into each costume", "the audience members watching the finished film in a cinema"], answer: 2 }, { q: "What can be inferred from the final paragraph about the narrator's expectations before visiting the studio?", options: ["The narrator had expected the studio to be much smaller and less organized than it actually was.", "The narrator had assumed that visiting a film studio would be boring and disappointing.", "The narrator had believed that digital technology and special effects were the main elements that made films convincing.", "The narrator had thought that only actors and directors were responsible for making a film feel realistic."], answer: 2 }] },
    { title: "The Growth of Online Learning", paragraphs: ["A decade ago, the idea that a student in rural Vietnam could complete a university-level course taught by a professor at a top American institution — without leaving home, without paying international tuition fees, and without sacrificing their existing work or family commitments — would have seemed far-fetched to most people in the education sector. Today, it is a routine possibility. The growth of online learning over the past ten years has been one of the most significant transformations in the history of education, reshaping not only how knowledge is delivered but who has access to it, and on what terms.", "The scale of this expansion is striking. Platforms such as Coursera, edX, and Khan Academy now collectively serve hundreds of millions of registered learners across more than 190 countries. Massive Open Online Courses — more commonly known as MOOCs — allow institutions including Harvard, MIT, and the National University of Singapore to offer free or low-cost versions of their courses to anyone with an internet connection. Certificate programs that once required relocation, years of study, and tuition fees in the tens of thousands of dollars can now be completed part-time, on a flexible schedule, and at a fraction of the traditional cost. For adult learners seeking to retrain for new careers, this flexibility has proven particularly transformative .", "The COVID-19 pandemic acted as an accelerant, forcing educational institutions at every level — from primary schools to research universities — to adopt online delivery at a pace and scale that would otherwise have taken years. School districts that had never experimented with remote learning were obliged to deploy digital platforms within weeks, and universities that had resisted online education on philosophical grounds found themselves offering entire degree programs through video conferencing software. When campus life resumed, many institutions chose to retain some form of blended learning — combining in-person and online elements — as a permanent feature of their educational model, having discovered that it offered students greater scheduling flexibility without necessarily compromising academic quality.", "Despite these advances, significant barriers to equitable access remain. The digital divide — the gap between those who have reliable access to high-speed internet and capable devices and those who do not — continues to exclude large proportions of potential learners, particularly in low-income countries and in rural and remote communities within wealthier nations. A student without a stable internet connection cannot meaningfully participate in a live video lecture, submit assignments through an online portal, or access digital course materials. [1] Efforts to bridge this gap through satellite internet initiatives and subsidized device programs have made some progress, but the scale of the remaining shortfall means that online learning's promise of universal access remains, for now, unrealized.", "Pedagogy presents a separate set of challenges. Critics of online learning argue that the format is poorly suited to subjects that depend on hands-on practice, laboratory work, collaborative problem-solving, or the kind of nuanced, responsive mentorship that experienced educators provide in person. Studies comparing outcomes in online and in-person settings have produced mixed results, with online learners tending to perform comparably on knowledge-recall assessments but showing weaker outcomes on tasks requiring higher-order thinking, sustained engagement, and interpersonal skills. Completion rates for MOOCs, in particular, remain stubbornly low — typically between 5 and 15 percent of registered participants — raising questions about whether the open-enrollment model generates genuine learning at scale or primarily the appearance of access. [2]", "Advocates of online education counter that these criticisms apply most forcefully to poorly designed courses and that well-structured online learning environments — those incorporating interactive elements, peer collaboration tools, timely instructor feedback, and adaptive assessment — can achieve outcomes comparable to the best in-person instruction. They also emphasize that the appropriate comparison is not between an idealized on-campus experience and an average online course, but between online learning and the alternative that most learners would otherwise face: no access to formal education at all. For the student in rural Vietnam completing a Harvard-designed course at midnight after a working day, the relevant measure of value is not whether the experience replicates campus life but whether it meaningfully expands her horizons. [3]", "The future trajectory of online learning will depend on how successfully the field resolves the tension between scale and quality, and between openness and rigor. Artificial intelligence tools that provide personalized feedback, track learner progress in real time, and flag disengagement before it leads to dropout hold significant promise. So do hybrid models that combine the flexibility of online delivery with the irreplaceable social and practical dimensions of in-person learning. What is already clear is that online education has permanently altered the architecture of opportunity in global education — and that the institutions, governments, and learners best placed to benefit from that change are those willing to engage with its possibilities and its limitations in equal measure. [4]"], questions: [{ q: "What is the main purpose of this passage?", options: ["To explain how the COVID-19 pandemic permanently destroyed the market for in-person higher education", "To argue that traditional university education is no longer relevant in the digital age", "To examine the growth and impact of online learning while acknowledging both its achievements and its remaining challenges", "To compare the academic quality of online courses with those offered by top universities on campus"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what do completion rate statistics for MOOCs suggest?", options: ["Most learners who register for MOOCs complete them and go on to earn recognized qualifications.", "Students who pay for a certificate are much more likely to complete a MOOC than those who enroll for free.", "Only between 5 and 15 percent of registered participants typically finish a MOOC, raising questions about genuine learning at scale.", "Completion rates have risen sharply over the past five years as course design has improved significantly."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what did many universities decide to keep after campus life resumed following the pandemic?", options: ["Some form of blended learning, combining online and in-person elements as a lasting part of their model", "Extended library access and digital resource subscriptions purchased during the lockdown period", "Fully online degree programs that replaced all in-person teaching permanently", "Video conferencing software as the primary tool for all student assessments"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, which group of learners does the passage say has found online learning's flexibility particularly valuable?", options: ["Young students completing secondary school qualifications in countries with limited school places", "International students who cannot afford to travel to study in another country for a short course", "Primary school children in rural areas who lack access to qualified teachers locally", "Adult learners who wish to retrain for new careers while managing existing work and personal commitments"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, how many countries do platforms such as Coursera, edX, and Khan Academy serve?", options: ["Fewer than 100", "More than 190", "Exactly 200", "Around 150"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a criticism of online learning?", options: ["Completion rates remain very low compared to the number of people who initially register", "Online qualifications are not recognized by most employers as equivalent to traditional degrees", "It does not provide the same quality of personalized mentorship as face-to-face instruction", "It is poorly suited to subjects requiring practical, hands-on work or laboratory experience"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 3 refers to _______.", options: ["the campus life that universities invited students to return to after the pandemic", "video conferencing software used to deliver degree programs during lockdowns", "blended learning, which institutions found offered students more scheduling flexibility", "the decision to adopt online delivery made by universities at the start of the pandemic"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"transformative\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["convenient and practical for people with limited time available", "capable of producing deep and fundamental change in a person's circumstances", "technically advanced and difficult to use without specialist training", "affordable enough to be within the financial reach of most working adults"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"trajectory\" in paragraph 7 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["the path or direction in which something is developing or moving", "the speed at which a new practice is adopted across different regions", "the financial investment required to develop a technology over time", "the criticism or opposition that a new idea faces from established institutions"], answer: 0 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'Without access to these tools, online education risks becoming yet another advantage reserved for those who are already relatively privileged.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "Developing Local Tourism", paragraphs: ["Tourism has long been recognized as a powerful engine of economic development, generating income, employment, and infrastructure investment in communities that might otherwise struggle to attract outside capital. Yet the relationship between tourism and local development is neither automatic nor uniformly beneficial. The outcomes depend critically on how tourism is planned, who controls its proceeds, and whether the activity is designed to integrate with — or simply extract from — the social and environmental fabric of the destination. In recent decades, a growing body of research and practice has coalesced around the concept of local tourism development: a model that seeks to maximize the share of tourism benefits retained within host communities while minimizing the negative externalities that mass tourism can impose.", "The economic case for localizing tourism proceeds is straightforward. In many conventional tourism arrangements, a significant proportion of visitor spending leaks out of the destination economy almost immediately. International hotel chains repatriate profits to their headquarters; tour operators based in origin countries retain commissions; and imported goods fill resort restaurants and gift shops rather than locally produced alternatives. Studies conducted across a range of developing-country destinations have estimated that the share of tourism revenue that remains within the local economy — the so-called \"local multiplier\" — can be as low as 20 to 30 percent in highly leaky tourism systems. [1] Community-based tourism initiatives, by contrast, channel visitor spending toward locally owned accommodation, food produced by nearby farmers, and cultural experiences led by resident guides, raising the local multiplier substantially and distributing benefits more broadly across the host population.", "Heritage and culture are among the most powerful draws for tourists seeking experiences that feel authentic and distinctive. Local tourism development strategies frequently center on the identification and careful presentation of a destination's unique historical, architectural, culinary, and artistic assets. [2] This approach requires a delicate balance: too little presentation leaves visitors without the context needed to appreciate what they are seeing, while over-commercialization risks hollowing out the very distinctiveness that made the destination attractive in the first place. Destinations that have managed this balance successfully — such as the ancient town of Hoi An in Vietnam or the medinas of Morocco — have typically done so by combining regulatory protection of core heritage sites with community-led interpretation programs that give residents an active voice in how their culture is represented.", "Environmental sustainability is an increasingly central concern in local tourism planning. The very landscapes, ecosystems, and natural attractions that draw visitors to a destination are precisely the assets most vulnerable to degradation through overuse, pollution, and physical impact. Carrying capacity frameworks — which set limits on the number of visitors a site can accommodate without irreversible damage — have been adopted by a growing number of protected areas and heritage sites worldwide. [3] The implementation of visitor fees, timed entry systems, and seasonally adjusted access policies allows destination managers to regulate pressure on sensitive environments while generating revenue that can be reinvested in conservation and community welfare. Critics of carrying capacity approaches argue, however, that enforcement is difficult in practice and that such measures can restrict access for domestic and lower-income visitors while having minimal effect on the high-spending international tourists who often cause the most environmental impact per visit.", "Community engagement is the dimension of local tourism development most consistently identified by researchers as the determinant of long-term success or failure. Where host communities are involved in planning decisions, share meaningfully in economic benefits, and retain the capacity to refuse or reshape tourism activities that conflict with their values or livelihoods, tourism tends to generate sustained positive outcomes. Where development is imposed from outside — by national governments, foreign investors, or international development agencies operating without genuine local participation — it frequently generates resentment, environmental degradation, and the gradual erosion of the social cohesion that makes a destination worth visiting in the first place. The principle of free, prior, and informed consent, borrowed from indigenous rights frameworks, has been adapted by some destination planners as a foundation for community engagement in tourism development contexts.", "The challenges of local tourism development are compounded in destinations that lack institutional capacity: reliable land use planning systems, transparent governance structures, trained hospitality and tourism professionals, and functioning infrastructure — roads, electricity, water — that can absorb visitor flows without collapsing under pressure. Many of the world's most compelling tourism destinations, particularly in sub-Saharan Africa, South and Southeast Asia, and parts of Latin America, face precisely these constraints, and the risk that development organizations and governments reach for tourism as a quick economic fix without investing in the foundational prerequisites for sustainable management is a recurring pattern in the development literature. [4]", "Despite these challenges, the evidence from well-implemented local tourism initiatives is broadly encouraging. Destinations that have invested in community capacity, protected environmental assets, and created inclusive economic arrangements have demonstrated that tourism can deliver genuine and lasting development dividends — not as a substitute for broader structural investment but as a complement to it. The critical insight that separates successful local tourism development from its more destructive alternatives is deceptively simple: the goal is not to bring as many visitors as possible to a place, but to ensure that the visitors who come leave the place better — not worse — than they found it."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central argument of the passage about local tourism development?", options: ["The success of tourism as a development tool depends on careful planning, community involvement, and ensuring that benefits remain within host communities.", "Heritage tourism is more sustainable than nature tourism because it relies on assets that cannot be physically depleted.", "Tourism should be discouraged in environmentally sensitive destinations regardless of potential economic benefits.", "International hotel chains and tour operators are the primary obstacle to equitable tourism development worldwide."], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the term \"local multiplier\" refer to in the context of tourism?", options: ["The proportion of tourism revenue that stays within the local economy rather than leaking out", "The rate at which a destination's visitor numbers grow from year to year", "The number of local jobs created for every 100 visitors who arrive in a destination", "The ratio of domestic tourists to international visitors in a given destination"], answer: 0 }, { q: "According to the passage, what have destinations like Hoi An and the Moroccan medinas done successfully in managing their heritage?", options: ["They have replaced traditional buildings with modern reconstructions that are easier to maintain and less vulnerable to tourist damage.", "They have restricted access to heritage areas entirely to prevent any commercial activity from taking place.", "They have combined legal protection of heritage sites with community-led programs that give residents a voice in cultural representation.", "They have partnered with international luxury hotel brands to attract high-spending tourists while protecting cultural sites."], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what is one criticism raised against carrying capacity approaches to managing tourist sites?", options: ["They are too expensive for governments in developing countries to implement and enforce effectively.", "They focus only on visitor numbers without addressing the environmental behavior of individual tourists.", "They have been shown to reduce tourism revenue so significantly that conservation programs lose their funding.", "They may limit access for local and lower-income visitors while barely affecting wealthier international tourists."], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, from which field was the principle of free, prior, and informed consent originally drawn before being applied to tourism planning?", options: ["International environmental law and biodiversity conservation frameworks", "Indigenous rights frameworks and the protection of native communities", "Urban planning theory developed in response to the displacement of low-income communities", "Corporate governance standards developed by multinational development banks"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a tool used to manage visitor pressure on sensitive environments?", options: ["Prohibiting the use of private vehicles within heritage zones and requiring shuttle transport", "Charging entrance fees to visitors who enter protected areas", "Allowing entry only during specific time slots rather than throughout the day", "Adjusting access policies according to the season to protect fragile ecosystems"], answer: 0 }, { q: "The word \"it\" in paragraph 5 refers to _______.", options: ["the principle of free, prior, and informed consent borrowed from indigenous rights frameworks", "tourism development imposed on communities from outside without genuine local participation", "the social cohesion that makes a destination attractive to international visitors", "community engagement, which researchers identify as the key factor in tourism success"], answer: 1 }, { q: "The word \"combining\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["replacing one approach with another that has proven more effective", "alternating between different strategies depending on the circumstances", "negotiating a compromise between two groups with opposing interests", "bringing two different elements together so that they work jointly"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"externalities\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["physical structures such as roads and airports that support tourist activity", "side effects or consequences experienced by people not directly involved in a transaction or activity", "regulations imposed by national governments on local tourism operators", "outside investors who bring capital but take profits away from local communities"], answer: 1 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'In some of the most visited destinations in Southeast Asia, this leakage has been estimated at over 70 percent of total visitor expenditure.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 0 }] },
    { title: "The Environmental Cost of AI Waste", paragraphs: ["The discourse surrounding artificial intelligence has been overwhelmingly preoccupied with questions of capability, safety, and economic disruption — with the environmental footprint of the hardware that makes AI possible receiving comparatively scant attention from either the technology industry or the broader public. Yet the proliferation of AI systems, from large language models requiring data centers housing hundreds of thousands of processors to the neural processing units embedded in consumer smartphones, is generating a category of material and energetic waste whose scale and composition pose challenges that existing regulatory and recycling infrastructure are poorly equipped to address. [1] Understanding this footprint requires attending not only to the carbon emissions associated with AI computation but to the physical lifecycle of the hardware itself — from the extraction of critical minerals used in chip fabrication to the eventual disposal of obsolete processors in conditions that expose both ecosystems and human communities to toxic contamination.", "The manufacture of AI processors — graphics processing units (GPUs), tensor processing units (TPUs), and the application-specific integrated circuits increasingly designed for neural network inference — is extraordinarily resource-intensive. A single advanced semiconductor fabrication plant, or fab, consumes volumes of ultra-pure water, industrial chemicals, and rare earth elements that would be considered remarkable in almost any other manufacturing context. The rare earth and critical minerals embedded in these chips — including cobalt, lithium, tantalum, and neodymium — are sourced predominantly from a small number of countries, several of which have documented histories of ecologically damaging extraction practices and inadequate protections for the workers and communities living near mining sites. The concentration of this supply chain creates both environmental vulnerability and geopolitical fragility that the AI industry has been slow to acknowledge .", "The energy demands of training and operating large-scale AI models constitute a second, more widely discussed dimension of the environmental problem. Estimates of the carbon footprint associated with training a single frontier large language model vary considerably depending on the energy mix of the data centers used, but figures cited in peer-reviewed literature have ranged from several hundred to several thousand metric tons of carbon dioxide equivalent — comparable, in some analyses, to the lifetime emissions of multiple passenger vehicles. [2] Data center operators have responded to growing scrutiny by publishing power usage effectiveness metrics and announcing commitments to renewable energy procurement, but critics note that absolute energy consumption continues to rise rapidly even as efficiency per computation improves, a dynamic sometimes described as a form of Jevons paradox — the tendency for efficiency gains in resource use to be offset by increases in total consumption driven by expanding demand.", "The disposal phase of AI hardware presents what may ultimately prove to be the most intractable environmental challenge. Unlike carbon emissions, which are at least partially amenable to mitigation through renewable energy transitions, the physical waste generated by obsolete AI hardware accumulates in material form. [3] The accelerating pace of AI model development creates pressure on operators of data centers and cloud computing facilities to upgrade their hardware at intervals that bear little relationship to the functional lifespan of the processors involved — a phenomenon that critics have termed \"AI-driven planned obsolescence.\" The majority of this discarded hardware enters global e-waste streams that are poorly regulated, with a substantial proportion ultimately processed in informal recycling sectors in West Africa, South and Southeast Asia, and Latin America, where workers extract valuable metals using methods — open burning, acid baths — that release highly toxic compounds into soil, groundwater, and air.", "The regulatory response to AI-related e-waste has been fragmented and, in most jurisdictions, inadequate. Extended producer responsibility schemes — which require manufacturers to finance the collection and recycling of their products at end of life — exist in some form in the European Union, Japan, and South Korea, but coverage of advanced AI hardware is inconsistent, enforcement is variable, and the pace of regulatory development has lagged far behind the rate of hardware proliferation. In the United States, e-waste regulation remains primarily a state-level patchwork, with no comprehensive federal framework mandating responsible disposal of AI-specific hardware. [4] Meanwhile, the voluntary sustainability commitments made by leading AI companies — typically framed around carbon neutrality targets and renewable energy procurement — have been criticized by environmental advocates as insufficiently attentive to the material waste dimension of AI's environmental impact, focusing on what can be measured and reported attractively while leaving harder problems inadequately addressed.", "Addressing the environmental costs of AI waste requires interventions at multiple points in the hardware lifecycle. At the design stage, extending the operational lifespan of AI processors through modular architectures that allow component-level upgrading rather than full system replacement could substantially reduce the volume of hardware discarded. At the procurement stage, requirements for verified responsible sourcing of critical minerals and transparency about supply chain environmental and labor practices would shift incentives for both manufacturers and purchasers. At the end-of-life stage, mandatory take-back programs, investment in formal high-value recycling infrastructure, and international agreements governing the transboundary movement of e-waste would begin to close the regulatory gaps that currently allow harmful disposal practices to persist at scale.", "The environmental costs of AI are not inevitable — they are the product of choices made about how hardware is designed, sourced, powered, and discarded, and of the degree to which those choices are subjected to meaningful regulation and public accountability. A technology industry that positions itself as a force for solving humanity's greatest challenges while externalizing the environmental costs of its own infrastructure onto vulnerable communities and ecosystems arguably undermines the very legitimacy of that claim. Whether the governance frameworks needed to align AI's material footprint with its stated ambitions can be developed at a pace commensurate with the technology's expansion remains one of the more consequential open questions in contemporary environmental policy."], questions: [{ q: "What is the central claim of the passage?", options: ["Technology companies have already made sufficient voluntary commitments to address the environmental costs of AI infrastructure.", "AI development should be halted until comprehensive international environmental regulations are in place.", "The environmental footprint of AI hardware — including resource extraction, energy use, and e-waste — is a serious and underaddressed problem that requires systemic intervention.", "Carbon emissions from AI data centers are a more serious environmental problem than the physical waste generated by discarded hardware."], answer: 2 }, { q: "What is the author's attitude toward the technology industry's current approach to AI's environmental impact?", options: ["Cautiously optimistic, noting that regulatory frameworks are developing at a pace sufficient to manage the problem", "Broadly supportive, noting that renewable energy commitments and efficiency improvements demonstrate genuine progress", "Critically skeptical, suggesting that industry efforts have focused on measurable and reputationally convenient metrics while leaving material waste problems inadequately addressed", "Entirely dismissive, arguing that all voluntary sustainability initiatives by AI companies are deliberate deception"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the term \"Jevons paradox\" describe in the context of AI energy use?", options: ["The tendency for companies to underreport energy consumption when publishing sustainability metrics", "The contradiction between AI companies' renewable energy pledges and their continued reliance on fossil-fuel-powered grids", "The pattern in which efficiency improvements in resource use are outweighed by rising total consumption driven by greater demand", "The observation that gains in computing efficiency per unit of output lead to falling total energy demand"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, what does the passage mean by \"AI-driven planned obsolescence\"?", options: ["Government policies in some countries that require AI hardware to be replaced and recycled at regular intervals for safety reasons", "The pressure on data center operators to replace hardware more frequently than its functional lifespan warrants, due to the rapid pace of AI model development", "A deliberate strategy by chip manufacturers to design processors that stop functioning after a set period to force upgrades", "The practice of software companies releasing AI model updates that are incompatible with older hardware to stimulate device sales"], answer: 1 }, { q: "According to the passage, in which regions does extended producer responsibility legislation for e-waste exist in some form?", options: ["China, India, and Brazil", "The United States, Canada, and Australia", "The United Kingdom, Singapore, and South Africa", "The European Union, Japan, and South Korea"], answer: 3 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following interventions is proposed as a design-stage solution to reduce AI hardware waste?", options: ["Building processors using only materials that can be fully recovered through conventional recycling methods", "Introducing international tariffs on the import of AI hardware from countries with poor environmental records", "Developing modular chip architectures that allow individual components to be upgraded without replacing the entire system", "Requiring AI companies to publish annual reports on the volume of hardware they discard each year"], answer: 2 }, { q: "According to the passage, which of the following is NOT mentioned as a proposed intervention for addressing AI hardware waste?", options: ["Mandatory programs requiring manufacturers to take back hardware at the end of its life", "Transparency requirements for the environmental and labor conditions in mineral supply chains", "A global tax on AI computation to fund environmental remediation in mining-affected communities", "International agreements to regulate the cross-border movement of electronic waste"], answer: 2 }, { q: "The word \"their\" in paragraph 4 refers to _______.", options: ["critics who have identified the phenomenon of AI-driven planned obsolescence", "AI model developers who release new versions at an accelerating pace", "workers in informal recycling sectors who process discarded AI processors", "operators of data centers and cloud computing facilities who must keep hardware current"], answer: 3 }, { q: "The word \"attentive\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to _______.", options: ["transparent and honest in the reporting of environmental performance data", "responsive and willing to change course when presented with new evidence", "ambitious in the scale of targets set for reducing environmental harm", "carefully focused on and mindful of a particular issue or concern"], answer: 3 }, { q: "Which of the following square brackets [1] , [2] , [3] , or [4] best indicates where the following sentence could be added? 'This gap reflects a broader pattern in which the technology sector's environmental commitments have been shaped more by reputational management than by the full scope of scientific evidence about material impact.'", options: ["[1]", "[2]", "[3]", "[4]"], answer: 3 }] },
  ] },
];
